S - Situation:
A significant and widespread outage has occurred in Google Cloud's us-central1 region. Multiple core services, including Compute Engine, Cloud SQL, and regional Cloud Storage buckets, are experiencing unavailability or severe degradation. Our applications and services hosted primarily in us-central1 are inaccessible to users, and critical business operations are impacted. Cloud Monitoring alerts and external status pages confirm the regional failure.
T - Task:
The primary task is to activate the Disaster Recovery plan to failover critical applications and services from us-central1 to our designated DR region, us-east1. The goal is to restore service availability within the defined RTO, minimize data loss to meet the RPO, and ensure business continuity. Once us-central1 is fully restored and stable, a failback plan will be executed.
A - Action:
The DR process involves several coordinated steps, leveraging specific Google Cloud tools:
Phase 1: Detection, Assessment, and Decision (Time: 0 - 30 minutes)
Automated Detection & Alerting:
Cloud Monitoring: Pre-configured alerts on key metrics (e.g., GCE instance health, Cloud SQL instance availability, API error rates for us-central1 services) trigger notifications.
Pub/Sub Alerts: Cloud Monitoring alerts are configured to publish messages to a specific Pub/Sub topic. This topic has subscriptions that notify:
The on-call SRE/DR team via PagerDuty/Slack integrations.
Automated systems that can initiate preliminary diagnostic checks.
Google Cloud Status Dashboard: Simultaneously, the DR team monitors the official Google Cloud Status Dashboard for confirmation and updates on the outage.
Impact Assessment:
The DR team convenes (virtually) to assess the scope and severity of the outage based on internal alerts and official Google Cloud communications.
Confirm that the issue is indeed regional and not isolated to our specific project or a transient network blip.
DR Plan Invocation:
Based on the assessment and confirmation of a prolonged regional outage, the designated DR Lead (e.g., Head of Infrastructure, Lead SRE) formally declares a disaster and invokes the DR plan. Communication is sent out to all relevant stakeholders.
Phase 2: Failover Execution (Time: 30 minutes - 1.5 hours)
Data Layer Failover - Cloud SQL:
Tool: Cloud SQL Cross-Region Read Replicas.
Action:
Our Cloud SQL primary instance in us-central1 has a cross-region read replica configured in us-east1.
The DR team initiates the promotion of the us-east1 read replica to a standalone, writable primary instance. This process typically takes a few minutes.
RPO is met because replication lag is generally low (seconds to a few minutes).
Configuration Update: Application configurations (potentially managed via Secret Manager or Config Connector) are updated to point to the new Cloud SQL primary instance's IP address/connection name in us-east1.
Object Storage Access - Cloud Storage:
Tool: Multi-Region Cloud Storage Buckets.
Action:
Critical static assets, user uploads, and backups are stored in Multi-Region Cloud Storage buckets (e.g., US multi-region).
These buckets remain accessible even if one of the constituent regions (us-central1) is down. Applications deployed in us-east1 can directly access data from these multi-region buckets without any failover step for the storage itself.
For any regional buckets in us-central1 that might contain non-critical or temporarily staged data, this data will be unavailable until us-central1 recovers. (Our strategy prioritizes multi-region for critical data).
Application & Compute Layer Failover - Compute Engine / GKE:
Tool: Instance Templates, Managed Instance Groups (MIGs) / GKE Regional/Autopilot clusters, Global Load Balancer.
Action (assuming GCE with MIGs for simplicity):
Pre-configured Instance Templates for our application servers exist in us-east1.
MIGs in us-east1 are scaled up from a minimal standby count (e.g., 0 or 1 instance for health checks) to the required capacity to handle production traffic. This can be manual or automated via scripts triggered by the DR invocation.
If using GKE, regional clusters in us-east1 would be scaled up, or new workloads deployed to existing us-east1 clusters.
Application Deployment: Container images are pulled from Artifact Registry (which is regional, so ensure images are also available in us-east1 or use a multi-region registry if available/appropriate for the artifact type). Applications are deployed/started on the new compute instances.
Traffic Redirection - Cloud DNS & Global Load Balancing:
Tool: Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer, Cloud DNS.
Action:
Our primary entry point is a Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer. It has backend services configured with instance groups in both us-central1 (primary) and us-east1 (DR, initially with low/no capacity).
As us-central1 backends become unhealthy, the Global Load Balancer automatically starts directing traffic to healthy backends in us-east1 once they are scaled up and pass health checks.
For non-HTTP(S) traffic or direct CNAME/A record dependencies, Cloud DNS records might need to be updated to point to resources in us-east1. This should be scripted for speed and accuracy.
Messaging - Pub/Sub:
Tool: Pub/Sub (Global Service).
Action:
Pub/Sub topics and subscriptions are global by default. Publishers and subscribers in us-east1 can continue to publish to and pull from the same topics/subscriptions.
Message storage policy might be relevant: If messages were configured to be stored only in us-central1, new messages would be stored according to the policy (e.g., geo-replicated if not restricted). Messages already acked or expired from us-central1 would not be an issue. Unacked messages in us-central1 specific storage might be delayed until the region recovers. We use default (global) message storage.
Validation & Communication:
The DR team performs smoke tests and critical functionality checks on the applications now running in us-east1.
Internal and external stakeholders are updated on the service restoration.
Phase 3: Recovery and Failback (Post us-central1 Restoration)
Monitoring us-central1 Recovery:
Continuously monitor official Google Cloud communications and our Cloud Monitoring dashboards for the full restoration and stabilization of us-central1.
Data Synchronization:
Once us-central1 is stable, set up replication from the current Cloud SQL primary in us-east1 back to a new instance in us-central1.
Allow time for the replica in us-central1 to fully catch up.
For Cloud Storage, if any new data was written to regional buckets in us-east1 (not best practice for this DR scenario), it would need to be synchronized back to us-central1 using gsutil rsync or Storage Transfer Service. (Our design relies on Multi-Region buckets to avoid this complexity).
Controlled Failback:
Schedule a maintenance window for failback.
Promote the Cloud SQL instance in us-central1 back to primary, and reconfigure the us-east1 instance as its cross-region replica. Update application configurations.
Gradually shift traffic back to us-central1 using the Global Load Balancer by increasing capacity in us-central1 MIGs and reducing capacity in us-east1.
Update any DNS records if they were changed.
Decommission DR Environment (Scale Down):
Once services are stable in us-central1, scale down compute resources in us-east1 to their standby state.
Post-Mortem and DR Plan Refinement:
Conduct a thorough post-mortem of the DR event.
Update the DR plan with lessons learned.
Schedule regular DR testing.
R - Result:
Initial Result (Post-Failover): Critical business services are successfully restored and operational in the us-east1 DR region. User access is re-established. The RTO of < 2 hours is met. Data loss is minimized to within the RPO of < 15 minutes, primarily limited by the Cloud SQL replication lag at the time of promotion.
Final Result (Post-Failback): Services are fully restored to the primary us-central1 region with normal operational parameters. The DR environment in us-east1 is returned to a standby state. The business has weathered the regional outage with minimal disruption. The DR plan is validated and refined based on the real-world event.
